i=A.b([new A.dz("time travel","Time Travel","\u23f0","TIME TRAVEL GENRE RULES:\n- Each time travel story must begin in a different historical era.\n- Choose the era randomly from a wide range of world history.\n- Do NOT default to ancient Egypt or reuse the same era repeatedly.\n- Possible eras include: ancient Greece, Roman Empire, Tang Dynasty China, Mayan civilization, Viking age, Renaissance Italy, Industrial Revolution, early America, signing of the Constitution, dinosaurs, and many others.\n- Include at least two fun, age-appropriate historical facts about the chosen era.\n- Do not repeat the same era within the same session unless the child chooses to return.\n- The time travel device can be anything imaginative: a compass, a pocket watch, a book, a door, a telescope, etc. Vary it each story.","","","default",!0),new A.dz("fairy tale","Fairy Tale","\ud83c\udf08","FAIRY TALE GENRE RULES:\n- The child stars in a classic fairy tale or story world selected as the DESTINATION.\n- Stay true to the spirit and setting of the chosen story \u2014 the child replaces or joins the main character.\n- Keep classic story elements: the magical object, the challenge, the helpful friend, the satisfying ending.\n- The child makes choices that the original character faced \u2014 but the outcome can vary based on what they choose.\n- Include magical details, vivid settings, and whimsical language appropriate to fairy tales.\n- If no classic story is selected, create an original fairy tale with a clear hero, challenge, and magical resolution.\n- Keep the tone warm, wondrous, and age-appropriate \u2014 brave but never scary, magical but grounded in story logic.\n- End with a satisfying resolution and a gentle moral or lesson woven naturally into the story.","","","default",!0),new A.dz("animal adventure","Animal Adventure","\ud83d\udc3e",'ANIMAL ADVENTURE GENRE RULES:\n\nHABITAT VARIETY (CRITICAL):\n- Every story must be set in a DIFFERENT habitat. Rotate through: ocean, coral reef, rainforest, savanna, arctic/antarctic, woodland, wetland/swamp, desert, mountain, freshwater river/lake.\n- Do NOT default to beach or tide pool repeatedly.\n- Choose the habitat randomly and commit to it fully.\n\nANIMAL VARIETY (CRITICAL):\n- Do NOT default to crabs, monkeys, dolphins, elephants, or lions.\n- Every story must feature at least ONE animal most children have never heard of.\n- Less common animals to consider: capybara, axolotl, pangolin, okapi, quokka, binturong, shoebill stork, narwhal, sun bear, kakapo, fossa, tardigrade, mantis shrimp, leafy sea dragon, star-nosed mole, and many others.\n- Familiar animals are fine as supporting characters but the STAR must be less common.\n\nANIMAL FACTS (REQUIRED):\n- Include at least ONE surprising true fact per turn about the featured animal.\n- Facts must be age-appropriate and genuinely surprising. Examples:\n  - "A group of monkeys is called a troop."\n  - "Capybaras are the world\'s largest rodents."\n  - "Mantis shrimp can punch faster than a bullet."\n- Weave facts naturally into the story \u2014 never as a lecture.\n\nSTORY ARC VARIETY:\n- Vary the story arc each time. Options: animal finding food, raising young, seasonal migration, escaping a predator safely, finding shelter, animal friendships across species, recovering from injury with a ranger\'s help.\n- Do NOT default to "child explores and meets cute animals."\n\nREALISM:\n- Animals behave realistically \u2014 no talking animals unless genre is fairy tale.\n- Show real animal behaviors: hunting, nesting, communicating, playing.\n- Conservation themes welcome but keep them hopeful, not scary.',"","","default",!0),new A.dz("athletic achievement","Athletic Achievement","\ud83c\udfc5","ATHLETIC ACHIEVEMENT GENRE RULES:\nSPORT VARIETY (CRITICAL):\n- Use the specific sport selected for this story \u2014 stay true to its real rules, positions, equipment, and setting.\n- Vary story arcs: first day at practice, big game day, learning a new skill, overcoming a mistake, helping a teammate, comeback from behind.\n- NEVER default to a generic sport \u2014 if the sport is soccer, use soccer terminology, positions, and situations.\n\nTONE AND VALUES:\n- Stories should feel energetic, encouraging, and confidence-building.\n- Emphasize effort, practice, and teamwork over raw talent.\n- Include realistic setbacks \u2014 a missed shot, a bad call, a moment of doubt \u2014 that the child overcomes.\n- Never shame failure \u2014 model resilience and trying again.\n\nCHOICES:\n- Choices should reflect real in-game or practice decisions \u2014 pass or shoot, try the new move or play it safe, encourage a teammate or focus on yourself.\n- Both options should feel reasonable and have natural consequences.\n\nFACTS:\n- Include one real sports fact per story \u2014 a record, a rule, a famous moment, or a training technique.\n- Weave it naturally into the action.","","","default",!0),new A.dz("favorite vacation","Favorite Vacation","\u2708\ufe0f","FAVORITE VACATION GENRE RULES:\n- The child is visiting a real destination with their family.\n- Begin with a vivid 2-3 sentence arrival scene using sensory details \u2014 what they see, hear, smell, and feel.\n- Include at least one real, surprising fact about the destination each turn \u2014 local food, wildlife, history, or culture.\n- Vary activities: exploring landmarks, trying local food, meeting locals, discovering nature, solving a small mystery.\n- Choices must feel like real vacation decisions \u2014 where to explore next, what to try, who to talk to.\n- Keep the tone warm, adventurous, and family-friendly.\n- NEVER repeat the same activity type in consecutive turns.\n- The story should end with a memorable moment the child will want to tell someone about.","","","default",!0),new A.dz("friendship","Friendship","\ud83d\udc6b","FRIENDSHIP GENRE RULES:\n- Stories explore the real joys and challenges of childhood friendships.\n- Situations should feel genuinely relatable: meeting someone new, resolving a misunderstanding, including someone left out, navigating hurt feelings, standing up for a friend.\n- Characters should feel like real kids \u2014 not perfect, but trying their best.\n- Every story should naturally model one positive social skill: listening, apologizing, sharing, including, speaking up kindly.\n- Choices must reflect real social decisions \u2014 what to say, how to help, whether to speak up.\n- NEVER make the right choice obvious \u2014 both options should feel reasonable so the child genuinely thinks about it.\n- Keep conflict age-appropriate and always resolvable \u2014 no bullying that escalates, no permanent falling out.\n- End with connection restored or strengthened, not just conflict avoided.","","","default",!0),new A.dz("school day","School Day","\ud83c\udfeb","SCHOOL DAY GENRE RULES:\n- Stories are set during a real school day \u2014 classroom, hallway, cafeteria, playground, gym, art room, library.\n- Weave real learning into the story naturally \u2014 a math puzzle, a science discovery, a history mystery, a reading challenge.\n- Include relatable school situations: a tricky assignment, a class project, a substitute teacher, a lost item, a surprising lesson.\n- Choices should feel like real school decisions \u2014 which subject to tackle, how to help a classmate, what to do at recess.\n- Teachers should be warm, encouraging, and occasionally surprising \u2014 not authority figures to fear.\n- Vary the setting each turn \u2014 do not stay in the same classroom the whole story.\n- Include one real educational fact per turn woven naturally into the story.\n- End with the child feeling capable, curious, or proud of something they learned or did.","","","default",!0),new A.dz("space travel","Space Travel","\ud83d\ude80","SPACE TRAVEL GENRE RULES:\nDESTINATION VARIETY (CRITICAL):\n- Every story must visit a DIFFERENT destination. Rotate through: planets, moons, nebulae, black holes, asteroids, space stations, galaxies, comets, and phenomena.\n- Do NOT default to Mars or the Moon repeatedly.\n- Commit fully to the chosen destination \u2014 its gravity, color, temperature, atmosphere, and unique features.\n\nSCIENCE FACTS (REQUIRED):\n- Include at least ONE surprising true space science fact per turn.\n- Facts must be age-appropriate and genuinely amazing. Examples:\n  - Neutron stars spin 700 times per second.\n  - A day on Venus is longer than a year on Venus.\n  - The Sun could fit one million Earths inside it.\n- Weave facts naturally into the story \u2014 never as a lecture.\n\nSTORY ARC VARIETY:\n- Vary arcs each time: scientific discovery, first contact with alien life, repairing the spacecraft, navigating a meteor shower, landing on a new world, finding signs of life.\n\nTONE:\n- Space should feel wondrous, vast, and full of possibility \u2014 never scary or hopeless.\n- Alien life (if present) should be curious and non-threatening \u2014 no hostile invasions.\n- Keep physics grounded but age-appropriate \u2014 no need to explain relativity, but gravity and atmosphere matter.","","","default",!0),new A.dz("interviews","Interviews","\ud83c\udfa4",'INTERVIEW GENRE RULES:\n- The child is a friendly TV or podcast host interviewing the selected guest.\n- The guest identity is provided as DESTINATION \u2014 use exactly that guest, no substitutions.\n- Claude prepares 2-3 simple, fun questions the child can ask \u2014 one per story turn.\n- Questions must be concrete and answerable: not "How did that make you feel?" but "What did you eat for breakfast on the moon?"\n- The guest gives a short, entertaining answer full of surprising facts or funny details.\n- If the guest is real (scientist, athlete, historical figure), include at least one true fact per turn.\n- If the guest is fictional, stay true to their character and story world.\n- The child always has agency \u2014 they choose the next question or can add their own.\n- Keep the tone fun, warm, and like a kids talk show \u2014 never a classroom quiz.\n- End the interview with the guest giving the child a fun souvenir or fun fact to remember them by.',"","","default",!0),new A.dz("bible stories","Bible Stories","\u271d\ufe0f","BIBLE STORIES GENRE RULES:\nHISTORICAL FAITHFULNESS (CRITICAL):\n- Tell the story as it is recorded in the Bible \u2014 do not alter core events, characters, or outcomes.\n- The child is a witness and participant \u2014 a young person present at the scene who observes and interacts, but does not change Biblical events.\n- Historical and cultural details should be accurate to the Biblical period \u2014 clothing, food, customs, landscape.\n\nSTORY STRUCTURE:\n- Begin with a vivid arrival scene at the Biblical location \u2014 what the child sees, hears, smells, and feels.\n- Introduce the key Biblical figures naturally as characters the child encounters.\n- Choices should let the child explore different aspects of the story \u2014 who to talk to, what to observe, where to go next \u2014 without altering the Biblical narrative.\n- Include at least one meaningful detail from the story each turn that brings the scripture to life.\n\nTONE:\n- Stories should feel reverent, warm, and wonder-filled.\n- Characters like Moses, David, Jesus, and others should feel real, human, and relatable \u2014 not distant or intimidating.\n- Age-appropriate throughout \u2014 avoid graphic violence even in Old Testament battle stories; focus on the courage, faith, and outcome.\n- End with a moment of reflection or a simple truth the child can carry with them.","","","default",!0),new A.dz("type your own story","Type Your Own Story","\ud83d\udcdd","TYPE YOUR OWN STORY RULES:\n- The child has provided their own story idea or premise \u2014 honor it fully and build on it enthusiastically.\n- Match the tone and energy of the child's idea \u2014 if they want silly, go silly; if they want adventurous, go adventurous.\n- Expand their idea with vivid details, interesting characters, and unexpected twists they would enjoy.\n- Choices should feel like genuine creative decisions that could take the story in meaningfully different directions.\n- Never override or redirect the child's core premise \u2014 if they say they want to be a dragon, they are a dragon.\n- Fill-in prompts should invite creative expression: completing a funny sound, naming a character, describing what something looks like.\n- Keep the story age-appropriate and safe, but otherwise give maximum creative freedom.\n- Celebrate the child's imagination explicitly \u2014 the story should feel like their creation, not a generic tale.","","","default",!0)],t.L5)
